00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
it's great to welcome to the program  today David Sinclair who's a professor  of genetics at Harvard Medical School as  well as the author of the new book  lifespan why we age and why we don't  have to David so great to have you on  today  hey thanks David thanks for having me on  so we've interviewed folks like Aubrey  de Grey and others and everybody seems  to apply sort of different language and  vocabulary sometimes to talking about  dealing with aging there's the idea of  curing aging there's the idea of  eliminating aging or making aging  obsolete as an example can you talk a  little bit about sort of your general  philosophy when it comes to the human  lifespan and the sort of way you talk  about and think about it sure well I'm  not anymore to list I'm a realist I'm a  scientist at Harvard Medical School  Brown Durden research we've been doing  for over 25 years now my view though is  that by tackling the major cause of all  diseases in our society all major  diseases which is aging I will have a  dramatic impact on those diseases and  people's longevity and how young they  can stay into the last 5 10 15 years of  their lives and how much of the focus in  your mind needs to be on or I guess when  we think about the approach that you  take is the idea to think about aging as  a condition that we are looking to treat  differently or is it more like for  example when we talk about cancer  treatment we're really talking about a  number of different conditions each each  which needs or requires sort of a  different approach depending on the  particulars the particulars being not  just the particulars of the condition  but also the particulars of the  individual that we're looking to treat  well about ten years ago the field that  I'm in longevity research we declared  that we had discovered the major  hallmarks of aging and these are things  like mitochondrial dysfunction of energy  decline stem cell depletion telomere  shortening  there's a group of them and we were  quite happy for those years but I've  always been fascinated in trying to find  what's causing all of those because they  don't just happen there has to be in my  view a unifying reason and that's what I  think that I've come up with this is my  best explanation and in the book I  described much of the evidence that  we've accumulated over 10 years so what  I think is causing all of those various  hallmarks is a loss of information the  analog information in ourselves now what  that means is that instead of having  five 10 20 different medicines to tackle  each one of the whole blocks of Aging  if I'm right a single medicine could  reverse the aging process and what is  the sort of physical way in which this  operates what what is it specifically  that is the process that you would be  looking to either undo stop or or or  actually reverse yeah well only in the  last few years we've realised that  across all mammals of course including  ourselves there's what's called the DNA  methylation clock we also call it the  horvath clock named after my friend  Steve Horvath and if I took your blood I  could read how old you are and even  predict when you're going to die and  these marks are part of the epigenome  now we all know the epigenome very well  but the epigenome is the layer above  that that regulates which genes are  turned on and off the epigenome is what  allows us to be to go from a fertilized  cell to a newborn of twenty six billion  cells each one of each tissue with  thousands of different types of cells in  there the epigenome I think is what goes  awry as we get older we lose the  epigenetic information and that's the  analog information of the cell now  anyone who's had a record player or a  cassette tape knows that analog  information is pretty useless at  preserving information and that I think  that's also true for our bodies now the  question is can you find a backup copy  of that epigenetic information  to perform a reset of the cells and I  think we've were coming very close to  being able to do that and we've already  got some some pretty interesting results  in mice already what's the mechanism  through which that could be done is it  the delivery mechanism I'm talking about  are we talking about a pill are we  talking about nanotechnology etc yeah  well it's early days in fact some of  this work is not even published yet  people listeners can find this online  we've posted it it's under peer-review  there have been others who have  published work that shows that you can  also reset the clock so how do we  currently do it well the first way it  was done was to genetically modified  mice that's obviously not going to help  and that was also toxic the way it  worked out we've now packaged a set of  three reprogramming genes that actually  we use what are called Yamanaka factors  Yamanaka genes Yamanaka won the Nobel  Prize in 2012 for learning how to make  stem cells out of skin cells and other  adult cells in the body but we use them  to reset the clock in the body we don't  make stem cells we push them partially  back to where they came from  we deliver them as you said in a virus  right now it's a viral therapy it would  be used we think in the eye initially  which is where the a lot of gene therapy  is being applied in a clinic right now  to correct genetic diseases so we  wouldn't be correcting genetic diseases  necessarily we'd be using it to rewind  the clock and allow tissues and organs  like the AI to recover and become more  youthful and in mice at least we've  managed to restore eyesight in old mice  what's the timeline that understanding  that it's still very early that you  think this may be developed on and  available on uh well I'm an entrepreneur  many people who know me know that very  well I've started many companies over  the years there's a new company that I'm  working with with Horvath a friend I  mentioned a professor at UCLA and myself  and professor Belmonte who did the  initial discovery that I'd mentioned  about  reprograming we've come together to to  make a go at this we're going to be  hopefully treating the first patient in  less than two years from now and our  goal is to treat glaucoma damage to the  eye due to pressure and we think that  that'll be if it works the first of many  treatments of turning back the clock in  various parts of the body and hopefully  eventually throughout the entire body to  have an age reset there are certain  populations that have been studied for  their longevity so there are the ik  Aryans there are you know a number of  these different populations that that  folks have heard of that live longer on  average than others can you talk a  little bit about whether in the work  that you do there is significance to  that my understanding is that much of  their longevity is attributed to  lifestyle it seems as though your work  is operating on a sort of different  level so do those populations have any  research value to you or are we really  dealing with something very very  different no actually it all ties  together you know if my theory didn't  explain the last hundred years of  observation and it wouldn't be a useful  theory so what we what we see and also  predict but we currently see is that a  stable epigenome the ability to slow  down that biological hook does happen  when societies and individuals have  those healthy lifestyles being hungry  every day or every other day  doing a lot of exercise keeping active  keeping the body in a state of high  alert these are the things that slow  down that epigenetic clock that I  believe is a driver if not the main  cause of aging itself now there are also  genes that we are either born of gene  variants that well we're born with if  we're lucky that predisposed to living  over a hundred physical centenarian  families and the variants in those  families are part of this epigenetic  regulatory network some of them are that  we work on a cold sirtuins and the eye  in that so  - and name stands for information  they're silent information regulators  and those are key players in this  epigenetic clock and we can use various  things in our lives such as what we eat  when we eat and how much exercise we do  among other things I put in my book to  slow down that rate of epigenetic clock  ticking there are many others that I  talk about in my book and other things  that can be done but we think these are  central regulators that are found  throughout life and have been with us  since the beginning of life do you have  a particular diet and/or supplement  regimen that you adhere to and the  reason I ask that is a lot of the folks  that work in the field that you work in  seem to have one which sometimes is not  obviously directly related to the  research that the person seems to be  doing so it's more of a curiosity  well yeah Matt mine's more than the  curiosity the work that we do in my lab  can be translated into my family's  lifestyle and what we take of course I  don't recommend to my family that they  take it but they're all scientists and  they know what the what the data said  suggests and the risks far far less than  the potential reward and so the first  discovery that we made was that these  sirtuin regulators of the epigenome need  a molecule called nad and so that was  back in actually in 2002 2003 so I take  nad boosting molecules that I I'm hoping  and the evidence suggests will put my  body in a state of high alert as though  I've been hungrier and in exercising the  right otherwise I don't have time before  I resveratrol this was in the news back  in 2003 we discovered that resveratrol  activates the sirtuin enzyme which is a  protector of the the body and also a  stabilizer of the epigenetic clock and  so resveratrol from Redwine I've been  taking that so as my father for over a  decade now  we both have very good health and so far  so good I should say by the way because  I'm a professor at Harvard Medical  School I know that this is not a  clinical trial it's not work that I'm  going to publish but I also know that  that we've all been born probably a  generation or two to early to know for  sure if these things work to extend  healthy lifespan and so we do have to be  ahead of the curve if we're going to  learn what these molecules are actually  doing that said all of these molecules  have been backed up by clinical trials  that I'm also running typically here at  Harvard and also around the world and we  know safety before any molecules are put  into my body um the last one we studied  was MIT form and we worked with Rafael  de Cobo at NIH to show that mice that  had metformin which is a diabetes drug a  reasonably safe one taken by millions of  people around the world that was  ostensibly extended their lifespan  certainly kept them healthier in human  studies it looks like in tens of  thousands of patients that have been  studied metformin slows down the aging  process prevents cancer heart disease  protein of course diabetes and for that  reason I take triple combo of those  things very interesting stuff and I  highly recommend that folks in the  audience pick up the book life span why  we age and why we don't have to we've  been speaking with the book's author  David Sinclair who's a professor of  genetics at Harvard Medical School  David really interesting having you on  and I so appreciate your time thanks  David I appreciate it too

